Subject: [PATCH] sycl: stop the XMX dequant GEMM and the gate + up MMVQ spilling on Arc A-series

Local fix. An acm-g10 AOT build showed register spills in kernels the A770 runs:

- The fused / grouped XMX dequant GEMM (prompt batches of the i-quants dense and MoE, the k-quants
  and q8_0 MoE): all 72 kernels, 1.9 to 3.5 KB per thread, the block and the reorder layout alike.
  Xe-HPG's sub-group-8 tiles are 8x8x16; a sub-group owned 16 rows x 32 columns, i.e. 8 f32
  accumulator tiles (64 of its 128 32-byte registers) plus four B tiles (32) and the A tile (8),
  before any addressing or dequantization. Neither the stack's AOT build (-device acm-g10) nor IGC's
  auto large-GRF mode (-ze-opt-enable-auto-large-GRF-mode, which C20's CMake adds) moves these
  kernels to 256 registers on acm-g10; forcing it (-ze-opt-large-register-file) does make the old
  tile spill-free, at half the threads per EU, which also halves the hiding of the A and B load
  latency (ZZ-LOCAL-0017 brings that tile back as an opt-in, grf_size<256>). Two changes, both for
  sub-group 8 only: a sub-group owns 8 rows (4 accumulator tiles), and the loop over the two k
  halves of a step is not unrolled, since unrolled IGC loads both halves' B tiles up front. Either
  alone still spilled (8 rows unrolled: 68 KB in total; 16 rows not unrolled: 121 KB). Together
  no kernel spills. BN stays 32, as the device-side MoE schedule requires (GGML_SYCL_FG_BN), so each
  dequantized A row still serves 32 columns; what is lost is B reuse, each B tile now feeding 8
  rows instead of 16. Measured on other shapes: 8 x 16 tiles do not spill either but dequantize A
  twice as often, and loading one B tile at a time made IGC spill more (126 KB).
- The reorder gate + up kernel pairs rows (two rows a sub-group sharing the activations): q4_K at 2..4
  columns (speculative-decoding verify), iq4_xs at 3..4 with candidate C10, q5_K at 3..5 where it
  takes this kernel, q4_K at 5..8 with candidate C30. At sub-group 16 that costs Xe-HPG twice the
  registers Xe2 pays (a value is two 32-byte registers there, one 64-byte one on Xe2), and every paired
  gate + up kernel spills on it: 0.5 to 3.6 KB for q4_K, 0.7 to 1 KB for iq4_xs, 1.1 to 3.5 KB for
  q5_K. On the A-series the gate + up kernel now always takes one row a sub-group; none of those
  kernels spills (q4_K at 8 columns keeps the 64 B it had). The plain MMVQ's row pairing, one weight
  matrix, does not spill on the A-series and is unchanged, as is every other arch.

Not changed: three DG2 decode kernels that spill one or two registers (q3_K reorder, one column:
32 B, one fill per block-loop iteration; q6_K six columns: 64 B; iq3_s reorder eight columns: 32 B),
and the XMX MMVQ kernels, which spill on DG2 but are gated to Xe2 and never run there.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-sycl/fused-gemm.cpp b/ggml/src/ggml-sycl/fused-gemm.cpp
index a9a6f841..ce5b818a 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-sycl/fused-gemm.cpp
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-sycl/fused-gemm.cpp
@@ -81,8 +81,12 @@ template <> struct fg_elem<mx::precision::tf32> {
 };
 
 // One joint_matrix combination (A type, B type, TM x TN x TK, sub-group size; C and D are f32) and
-// the tiling built on it. A sub-group owns SG_ROWS rows of A (at least 16) and BN (at least 32)
-// columns of B. A and B may differ: the device lists the pairs it supports.
+// the tiling built on it. A sub-group owns SG_ROWS rows of A (at least 16, 8 at sub-group 8) and BN
+// (at least 32) columns of B. A and B may differ: the device lists the pairs it supports.
+// Sub-group 8 is Xe-HPG (Arc A-series). IGC builds these kernels with 128 registers of 32 bytes there
+// (auto large-GRF mode does not switch them, and 256 would halve the threads per EU), and 16 x 32
+// tiles of 8x8 f32 accumulators, their B tiles and the A stage needed more: every kernel spilled 1.9
+// to 3.5 KB per thread. 8 rows keep BN, so each dequantized A row still serves 32 columns.
 template <typename TA, typename TB, int TM_, int TN_, int TK_, int SG_> struct fg_combo {
     using ta  = TA;
     using tb  = TB;
@@ -95,7 +99,7 @@ template <typename TA, typename TB, int TM_, int TN_, int TK_, int SG_> struct f
     static constexpr int TK = TK_;
     static constexpr int SG = SG_;
     static constexpr int VNNI    = 4 / sizeof(tsb);  // K rows of B packed in one 32-bit word
-    static constexpr int SG_ROWS = TM > 16 ? TM : 16;
+    static constexpr int SG_ROWS = TM > 16 ? TM : (SG == 8 ? 8 : 16);
     static constexpr int RPL     = SG_ROWS / SG;     // A rows one lane decodes per k step
     static constexpr int MT      = SG_ROWS / TM;
     static constexpr int BN      = TN > 32 ? TN : 32;
@@ -941,7 +945,9 @@ static void fused_dequant_gemm_tile(
             }
             sycl::group_barrier(sg);
 
-#pragma unroll
+            // at sub-group 8, unrolling the two k halves has IGC load both halves' B tiles up front,
+            // which spills even the 8-row tile
+#pragma unroll(S::SG == 8 ? 1 : FG_BK / S::TK)
             for (int kt = 0; kt < FG_BK / S::TK; ++kt) {
                 const int kq0 = (kb * FG_BK + kt * S::TK) / S::VNNI;
                 mx::joint_matrix<sycl::sub_group, TB, mx::use::b, S::TK, S::TN, S::b_layout> sub_b[S::NT];
diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-sycl/mmvq.cpp b/ggml/src/ggml-sycl/mmvq.cpp
index 4afe61b6..60efc334 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-sycl/mmvq.cpp
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-sycl/mmvq.cpp
@@ -3443,6 +3443,17 @@ bool ggml_sycl_mul_mat_vec_q_glu_plain(enum ggml_type gate_type, enum ggml_type
     return false;
 }
 
+// Row pairing in the gate + up kernel (two rows a sub-group, sharing the activations) needs twice the
+// registers. At sub-group 16 a value takes two of Xe-HPG's 32-byte registers, one of Xe2's 64-byte ones,
+// so on the A-series every paired gate + up kernel spills: 0.5 to 3.6 KB per thread for q4_K (2..8
+// columns, 5..8 from candidate C30), 0.7 to 1 KB for iq4_xs (candidate C10), 1.1 to 3.5 KB for q5_K.
+// The plain MMVQ's row pairing, one weight matrix, does not spill there and is left alone.
+static bool ggml_sycl_glu_row_pair(int device) {
+    const gpu_arch arch = ggml_sycl_info().devices[device].hw_info.arch;
+    return arch != gpu_arch::intel_gpu_acm_g10 && arch != gpu_arch::intel_gpu_acm_g11 &&
+           arch != gpu_arch::intel_gpu_acm_g12;
+}
+
 template <ggml_type type, int... Ns>
 static bool mul_mat_vec_q_glu_reorder_ncols(enum ggml_glu_op glu_op, const void * vx, const void * vgate,
                                             const void * vy, float * dst, int ncols, int nrows, int ncols_dst,
@@ -3454,6 +3465,13 @@ static bool mul_mat_vec_q_glu_reorder_ncols(enum ggml_glu_op glu_op, const void
         if (ncols_dst != n) {
             return false;
         }
+        // one row a sub-group where pairing spills, whatever width the pairing below would take
+        if constexpr (reorder_vec_dot_shared_activations<type>::value && n >= 2) {
+            if (!ggml_sycl_glu_row_pair(ggml_sycl_get_device())) {
+                launch_mul_mat_vec_q_reorder_glu_impl<vec_dot, n, 1>(vx, vgate, vy, dst, ncols, nrows, stride_col_y_bytes, stride_col_dst, glu_op, stream);
+                return true;
+            }
+        }
         if constexpr (type == GGML_TYPE_Q4_K && n == 2) {
             if (nrows >= Q4_K_MMVQ_ROW_PAIR_MIN_NROWS) {
                 launch_mul_mat_vec_q_reorder_glu_impl<vec_dot, 2, 2>(vx, vgate, vy, dst, ncols, nrows, stride_col_y_bytes, stride_col_dst, glu_op, stream);
